menu "SD Logger"

# =====================================================================
#  These are component-local on purpose, unlike the task priorities.
#
#  Queue depth and buffer size only affect sd_logger's own behavior and
#  RAM footprint -- nothing outside this component needs to reason about
#  them. Priorities went in main/Kconfig.projbuild instead because they
#  are only meaningful relative to every other task in the system.
# =====================================================================

config SD_QUEUE_MARGIN_MS
    int "Queue depth margin beyond one flush interval (ms)"
    range 0 5000
    default 500
    help
        Each of the three sensor streams now has its OWN queue
        (previously one shared queue for all three -- a burst from a
        fast sensor could evict a slower sensor's still-unwritten data
        purely by sharing capacity with it). Depth is no longer a
        single number you set directly; each stream's depth is derived
        from ITS OWN rate: rate_hz x (SD_LOGGER_FLUSH_MS + this margin)
        / 1000. See BARO_QUEUE_DEPTH/IMU_QUEUE_DEPTH/GPS_QUEUE_DEPTH in
        sd_logger.h for the exact formula and resulting numbers.

        Raising this gives every stream more headroom to absorb a card
        stall beyond one flush interval, at the cost of RAM
        proportional to that stream's own rate (IMU costs the most per
        added millisecond of margin, since it publishes fastest).

        Check sd_logger_diag_t.queue_full_drops after a flight -- if
        nonzero, raise this rather than guessing.

config SD_LOGGER_BUF_SIZE
    int "Per-stream write buffer (bytes)"
    range 128 4096
    default 512
    help
        Bytes buffered per stream before touching the card. There are
        three streams, so total cost is 3x this value.

        512 keeps any single write within one SD sector. It does NOT
        guarantee flushes land on aligned sector boundaries -- flush
        sizes vary by record size and rarely divide evenly into 512,
        so most flushes are smaller than a full sector and drift out
        of alignment over time. Unaligned writes cost the card an
        extra internal read-merge-write; this is real but small next
        to the stall tolerance this queue already provides. Padding to
        force alignment was considered and rejected: it would bloat
        the GPS file (~36 real bytes per second padded to 512, over
        90% waste) for a benefit smaller than what this buffer already
        absorbs. Keep this at 512 unless you have measured a specific
        throughput problem.

config SD_LOGGER_FLUSH_MS
    int "Periodic flush interval (ms)"
    range 100 60000
    default 1000
    help
        Every stream is flushed at least this often even if its buffer
        is not full.

        This exists for the slow streams: GPS at 1 Hz writes 36 bytes
        per fix, so a 512-byte buffer would take roughly 14 minutes to
        fill on its own. Without a periodic flush, a power cut would
        lose every GPS fix since the last one.

        Lower means less data at risk on a sudden power loss, at the
        cost of more frequent (and more wearing) card writes.

config SD_LOGGER_STACK_WORDS
    int "Writer task stack (words, not bytes)"
    range 2048 8192
    default 4096
    help
        FreeRTOS stack sizes on ESP-IDF are in 32-bit WORDS, so the
        default 4096 is 16 KB of actual RAM.

        The writer task calls into FATFS and the C standard library's
        buffered I/O, both of which are more stack-hungry than the
        sensor drivers. Verify with uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark() in
        your own build before trimming this.

endmenu
